{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does the relationship between a person’s political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy or uptake depend on the political climate where that person lives?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates\nthe relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine\nhesitancy and uptake.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the same pattern hold for attitudes and reported behavior, and for initial vaccination and boosters?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, by looking at both COVID vaccine and booster status and hesitancy, we\nidentify how attitudes and behaviors may have shifted as we transitioned from initial vaccine doses to boosters.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Regional politics mattered more for conservatives than for liberals: a more liberal local climate was associated with less hesitancy and more boosting among conservatives. This difference by political orientation did not extend to receipt of the initial vaccine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our findings indicate that political climate, as represented through regional 2020 presidential election votes,\nhas a stronger association with vaccine and booster hesitancy for individuals who are higher on political conservatism as\ncompared to those who are politically liberal.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "This same trend was observed for self-reported booster status, but not self-­\nreported vaccine status.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "By looking at political\norientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more\nlikely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors argue that understanding why conservatives’ uptake varies across regions could inform efforts to increase annual vaccination.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Identifying the specific mechanisms through which this happens would have significant implications for increasing\nvaccine uptake among politically conservative individuals across the US.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "These results have significant\nimplications for populations to target to increase COVID-19 vaccine uptake as we move towards annual vaccinations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors used a U.S. online convenience survey conducted through Qualtrics Panels from March 8 to April 19, 2023.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This was an online self-reported study in which a nonprobability convenience sample\nwas recruited from the United States between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were a convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels, an online research platform that manages\nparticipant recruitment and data quality for academic studies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey came from a project on marginalization. Recruitment targeted equal numbers of Black and White participants, split each racial group at $70,000 annual income, and requested a 60% cap on any one gender in each quota group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data used in this study were part of a larger project assessing the impact of racial and socioeconomic marginalization on COVID-19 vaccine attitudes and behaviors [27].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Half of the sample was requested to be White (and no other racial identity), and half the sample was requested\nto be Black (and no other racial identity). Within each racial group, half of the participants were requested to be below\nthe median annual income in the US [$70,000 [28]].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, within each quota group it was requested that no more than 60% of the\nparticipants be of any gender.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Of 1,777 respondents, 970 failed panel quality screens and nine more were excluded for incoherent answers. Excluding 115 without a usable zip code or matching election result left 683 participants.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Qualtrics Panels collected data from 1777 participants, and identified 970 participants as not passing all quality screens\n(described above). We eliminated an additional nine participants whose open-ended responses did not make logical\nsense, resulting in a sample of 798 validated participants.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We eliminated an additional 115 participants who did not provide valid zip code data (N = 100) or whose zip code did not have corresponding election data (N = 15). The final sample\nfor these analyses included 683 participants.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants were 18 to 94 years old. Of the sample, 519 reported initial vaccination and 365 reported at least one booster.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants ranged in age from 18 to 94 years (M = 46.29, SD = 18.94).",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "519 (76%) of participants reported receiving the initial COVID-19 vaccine and 365 (53%) reported\nreceiving at least one COVID-19 booster.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Individual conservatism was the average of two self-ratings, one on social issues and one on economic issues, each scored from 0 for strongly liberal to 10 for strongly conservative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“social issues (for example, abortion, gun rights, gay rights)”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "“economic issues (for example, taxation, government spending)”. Both items were rated on a 11-point sliding scale from\nstrongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The local-climate measure was the Republican share of all votes in the 2020 presidential election. The authors matched election data to respondents’ zip codes, averaging the data when a zip code matched multiple FIPS codes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided\nby participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times [22].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Political climate for each participant was determined by matching FIPS codes to corresponding zip\ncodes. In cases where a zip code included multiple FIPS codes, data was averaged across these FIPS.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we calculated the percent of Republican votes by dividing the number of Republican votes by the\ntotal number of votes (multiplied by 100).",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Separate survey questions asked whether respondents had received a vaccine and whether they had received a booster, defined as a dose after the initial series.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-reported vaccine status and booster status were assessed through individual items, “have you received a COVID19 vaccine” and “have you received a COVID-19 booster”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "The term “booster” is used in this analysis as this was the\ncommon term at the time to refer to subsequent doses of the COVID-19 vaccine after the initial vaccine series. Possible\nresponses were yes, no, and I don’t know.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The researchers adapted seven adult-vaccination attitude items to measure COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and used items from a 2022 survey to measure booster hesitancy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Vaccine hesitancy (i.e., negative attitudes toward vaccination) was measured\nusing the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale [24], which assesses the perceived value of\nvaccines in general.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "We modified the items to assess attitudes specifically toward the COVID-19 vaccine",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Booster hesitancy was\nassessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy [25].",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked both hesitancy scales with confirmatory factor analysis and reliability tests. Both had a Cronbach’s alpha of .93, and the items were averaged within each scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We performed confirmatory factor analysis on the items for the vaccine and booster hesitancy scales (S2 File) and the\nsubsequent reliability of the scales was calculated using Cronbach’s alphas.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The vaccine hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95% CI [.92,.93]) and were\naveraged to form the vaccine hesitancy scale. The booster hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95%\nCI [.93,.94]) and were averaged to form the booster hesitancy scale.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For unadjusted comparisons, the authors used Pearson correlations, t-tests, and chi-square tests to examine associations among survey measures and differences by vaccination and booster status.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Pearson correlations were used to assess\ncorrelations between all non-nominal variables (i.e., age, income, education, perceived vaccine access, vaccine and\nbooster hesitancy, political conservatism, and conservative regional climate).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "differences in\nfrequencies of vaccine status and booster status by gender and race were assessed using X2. Independent samples\nt-tests were used to assess differences in all non-nominal variables across vaccinated and unvaccinated participants.\nThe same analyses were run to assess differences between boosted and unboosted participants.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The moderation test used linear regression for hesitancy and logistic regression for receipt. The models added the two political measures and then their interaction to controls for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived access. Cases with missing model data were excluded.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with\nvaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between\npolitical conservatism and conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "same process was used with booster hesitancy as the outcome variable",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we ran two sets of generalized linear models with logit link\nand model-­based standard errors predicting vaccine status and booster status from the same three models described\nabove. For all analyses, any participants with missing data on any of these variables were excluded from the analysis.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "In adjusted analyses, personal conservatism and a more conservative region each predicted greater hesitancy about both kinds of dose and lower reported receipt of both.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were both associated with higher levels of COVID-19 vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower rates of self-reported vaccination and boosting, when controlling for age, education, income, and race.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Initial-vaccine hesitancy was low among liberals across regions. The increase in hesitancy with personal conservatism was steeper in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.\nAs political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals\nwho live in a more conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For boosters, conservatives were more hesitant in conservative regions than in liberal ones. Among the most liberal respondents, the small regional difference went in the opposite direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "There was a similar pattern for booster hesitancy, with more politically\nconservative individuals in a conservative regional climate having higher booster hesitancy than conservative individuals\nin a liberal regional climate. For the most politically liberal individuals, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regional climates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The estimated likelihood of boosting exceeded 65% for the most liberal respondents regardless of climate. For the most conservative respondents, it was 60% in liberal regions versus under 35% in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less\nthan 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For receipt of the initial vaccine, there was no significant interaction between individual and regional politics (OR 1.02, p = .833). The authors suggest mandates as a possible explanation for why uptake differed from hesitancy in this respect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political conservatism\nand conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833).",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this\ndifference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine\nmandates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study did not identify the mechanism behind the regional pattern. Social pressure is a possibility, not an established explanation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we did not asses the mechanism through which this occurs, these\nfindings suggest that environmental factors (e.g., social pressure) may impact decisions to receive annual COVID-19\ndoses.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Election returns are a proxy for political climate. Other differences between zip codes, such as population density, could account for the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that\nother zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population\ndensity) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Controlling for demographics did not test whether associations differed by subgroup. The pooled estimates are sample averages that could mask subgroup patterns, including a reversal such as Simpson’s paradox.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analyses controlled\nfor demographic covariates commonly associated with vaccine hesitancy, we did not formally assess effect modification\nby these factors.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Consequently, the reported pooled associations should be interpreted as average effects across the\nsample, and we cannot exclude the possibility that heterogeneity across demographic subgroups could produce aggregation effects such as Simpson’s paradox.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reported vaccination may differ from actual vaccination because respondents may misremember or give a socially desirable answer.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine\nstatus reflects actual vaccine status.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Online panel recruitment excluded people without access to the platform.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes\nparticipants with access to this platform.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Black and White panel sample may not represent the broader U.S. population.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through\nQualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional design establishes neither causation nor the direction of the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, it should be noted that this\ndata is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors combine and extend two strands of empirical research on the politics of vaccination: one focused on individual ideology, the other on regional voting patterns. They accept the earlier associations and ask what is missed when the two are studied separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our research is consistent with previous findings that conservative political orientation and a conservative\npolitical climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19\nvaccine [6,7,10,19].",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact\nof both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has\nbeen little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local\npolitical climate.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "The present research examines political climate as a potential moderator of the\nassociation between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and\nuptake.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Hao and Shao found that states with a larger Democratic presidential vote share in 2020 had higher booster rates in 2022. The present authors build on that aggregate finding by asking whose booster uptake varies with local political climate.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Likewise, state-level 2020 Democratic presidential voting\nshare was associated with higher state-level COVID-19 booster rates in 2022 [20].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Hao F, Shao W. Social network, political climate, income inequality, and Americans uptake of monovalent COVID-19 booster.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
